## Package comparison

The Phase 5 package remains aligned with the approved plan. It stays within singleton startup, listener-first server ownership, bounded Effect scheduling, and terminal skew handling.

## Findings

### Critical — Production listener-first shutdown still deadlocks on blocked coordinator work

`packages/music-core/session/server.ts:665-724` still awaits connection children before closing the selected coordinator scope. A connection forwarding from a blocked coordinator worker can wait for that worker, while the worker is interrupted only by the later `closeCoordinator`. The blocked-sampling fixture still bypasses the selected graph, so the green suite does not exercise this production cancellation cycle. Redesign the scopes and run the regression through the actual post-bind coordinator graph.

### High — Startup operation errors lose their required structured identity

The new `promise` adapter at `packages/music-core/session/client.ts:717-725` wraps every rejected discovery, cleanup, acquisition, launcher, and release Promise as `MusicSessionStartupError(operation: "timeout")`. Consequently a launcher's typed `operation: "spawn"` failure is changed to `timeout`, and tagged runtime/cleanup errors are also hidden. The package requires spawn, unsafe runtime, occupied, incompatibility, and config failures to remain terminal with their stable operation/details; only schedule exhaustion should become timeout. Preserve existing typed failures rather than indiscriminately translating them.

### High — The Effect workflow is not externally composable or interruption-safe

`connectOrStartMusicSession` constructs the Effect and immediately calls `Effect.runPromise`; there is no Effect workflow API that tests/callers can provide with `TestClock` or interrupt under a scope. Marker acquisition and `Ref.set(lease, ...)` are also separate interruptible steps (`client.ts:765-769`): interruption after exclusive acquisition but before recording the lease lets the finalizer observe `undefined` and leak a live marker. Expose the Effect workflow with a thin Promise adapter and make lease acquisition plus finalizer registration atomic/uninterruptible as required.

### High — Timing and release-finalizer policy remain incomplete

`startupSchedule` still has a fixed 25 ms exponential delay with no validated capped maximum or config boundary. The finalizer at `client.ts:795-803` deliberately ignores release failure, so it neither reports a secondary release error nor preserves both outcomes. Add validated initial/max/attempt settings, capped jittered scheduling, and explicit primary-plus-release error retention.

### High — Required acceptance evidence remains missing

No tests were added for the new workflow. The suite still lacks deterministic `TestClock` pacing/exhaustion/interruption, spawn-failure marker release/no retry, 20-way singleton convergence, a true bind race, and incompatibility before/after acquisition and while waiting.

